MODULAR ARITHMETIC
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When a problem says "return the answer modulo 10⁹ + 7",
the true answer is astronomically large. You compute it modulo
a prime to keep it manageable.

  const int MOD = 1e9 + 7;   // 1000000007 — almost always this value

The four rules:

  (a + b) % m  =  ((a % m) + (b % m)) % m        // addition
  (a - b) % m  =  ((a % m) - (b % m) + m) % m    // subtraction — add m to prevent negative
  (a * b) % m  =  ((a % m) * (b % m)) % m        // multiplication
  (a / b) % m  =  (a * modInverse(b, m)) % m     // division — covered below

  // CRITICAL: subtraction without +m gives wrong negative result
  long long diff = ((a % MOD) - (b % MOD) + MOD) % MOD;

  // Apply mod at EVERY step, not just the end
  long long ans = 1;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      ans = (ans * arr[i]) % MOD;   // correct
  }
  // NOT: ans *= arr[i]; at the end ans % MOD; — overflows before you get there

  // Multiplication needs long long even when inputs are < MOD
  // because (10^9) * (10^9) overflows int and even long long barely fits
  long long product = (1LL * a * b) % MOD;
